{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/PROBE-DESIGN.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe-targets.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/PROBE-DESIGN.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe-targets.json'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Paid acceptance probe of judging-v12 (design v3, D715; registered before any send)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Alex's ceiling:** $0.50 (D715, \"0.5 美元 (推荐)\"), held by `budget_replay.SharedLedger`, which stops at it.\n     4\t\n     5\t**Frozen inputs:**\n     6\t- `probe-plan.json` (`probe_plan.py`, $0);\n     7\t- `probe-targets.json`: the Part B targets, read from the stored refused answers before any send and never edited after the first.\n     8\t\n     9\t**Model and settings:** poc-v121's rejudging setting (gpt-6-luna, medium effort, 16,000 output tokens), the provider's strict format.\n    10\t\n    11\tv2 answers council round 40 (`codex-r40.md`):\n    12\t- blind reading rules, an omitted material fact counted;\n    13\t- all Part C comparisons read;\n    14\t- the pass line stated as this probe's tolerance;\n    15\t- completeness required;\n    16\t- the sealed request last;\n    17\t- a schedule proved against the real reservations;\n    18\t- request-time checks;\n    19\t- a full $0 rehearsal.\n    20\t\n    21\tv3 answers round 40b (`codex-r40b.md`):\n    22\t- the targets' sources completed against the stored accepted retries (e3768 for the signed agreement; e41 and e120 for the Kyber\n    23\t  delay; the AMD entries), with a `partial` list;\n    24\t- misattribution counted by claim;\n    25\t- a material fact unsupported in all three draws treated as omitted;\n    26\t- Part C reads what every answer says and attributes;\n    27\t- a refused v7 control answer is still read;\n    28\t- the budget claim corrected;\n    29\t- the stops rehearsed.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 1. What it decides\n    32\t\n    33\t| Question | Part |\n    34\t|---|---|\n    35\t| Q1. Does the provider accept judging-v12's format on a real request, the largest of the five archives included, and on an empty list? | A |\n    36\t| Q2. On the requests the open format let the model get wrong, does it now state each fact and cite a listed source for it, without a wrong listed id in its place, and without dropping a material fact? | B |\n    37\t| Q3. On ordinary requests, does the closed format change what the judge cites or concludes, beyond its own variation under the open format? | C |\n    38\t\n    39\t## 2. The sends, in this order\n    40\t\n    41\t1. **A1:** old-1 call 5652, the largest stored request of the five archives (451 evidence ids). Its `cites_views` is empty, so it\n    42\t   also exercises `maxItems: 0`.\n    43\t2. **A0:** old-3 call 69, a request with an empty theme list (`theme_pushes` with `maxItems: 0`); under $0.001.\n    44\t3. **Part B:** the 16 distinct refused requests, 3 sends each under v12's format.\n    45\t   - control-1's 8941 is 8938's retry: one request, both attempts' bad ids targeted.\n    46\t   - linked-1's 7 requests keep their stored judging-v8 text; only the answer format changes. That makes them a format probe on\n    47\t     those texts. No v8-based version is built.\n    48\t4. **Part C:** 12 matched comparisons, one per (source, question) of the refused set. Each is the same question's accepted\n    49\t   first-attempt request in the same archive with the closest evidence count; ties go to the nearest call on either side, which is\n    50\t   what `probe_plan.py` does.\n    51\t   - Each comparison is 3 sends under v12's format and 3 under judging-v7's open format (the fresh control).\n    52\t   - Comparisons run cheapest first. Within one comparison the six sends run one after another.\n    53\t   - The 16 refused requests map to 12 (source, question) pairs. The four duplicates are dropped to fit the ceiling, so every\n    54\t     question is covered once per source.\n    55\t5. **A2,** last and only if the headroom then covers its reservation ($0.0824): the sealed D358 run's call 6918 (647 ids) with v12's\n    56\t   format built from its lists. It is a size check on an older request text. A 200 shows that a request this size fits; a 400\n    57\t   establishes an enum limit only if the provider's error says so. Its answer is not read.\n    58\t\n    59\t**Start rule** (proved at $0 with `budget_replay.llm_estimate`):\n    60\t- Only one Part C comparison runs at a time.\n    61\t- It starts only when the ceiling minus spent minus reserved covers the sum of its six first attempts' reservations.\n    62\t- On the stored-token estimate all 12 start: lowest margin $0.037; expected core spend $0.347.\n    63\t- If every send costs 30% more than stored, only 10 start in this order: the 11th needs $0.136 with $0.107 left.\n    64\t- A retry reserves again, so an admitted comparison can still stop incomplete.\n    65\t- A comparison not started, or stopped, makes Part C incomplete, and the outcome inconclusive.\n    66\t\n    67\tEach part's sends run at most 4 at a time. Every send gets its own archive (per part, request, format and draw), so `model.call`\n    68\tnever answers a draw from an earlier draw: every draw is a fresh provider call, counted at the transport.\n    69\t\n    70\tA transport error is recorded. The budget transport retries a 429 or 5xx as it does in production, and settles an error at its\n    71\treservation.\n    72\t\n    73\t## 3. Reading rule (frozen)\n    74\t\n    75\t### Checks on every answer\n    76\tEvery answer is checked as at request time, with a harness check proved equal to `check_answer` on fixtures and on the stored\n    77\tanswers (§4):\n    78\t- membership against the request's lists;\n    79\t- the rung range from the request's ladder line;\n    80\t- first-judgement and changed against the request's \"[Previous conclusion …]\" line;\n    81\t- non-empty stance fields;\n    82\t- duplicate theme pushes.\n    83\t\n    84\tThe archive's final ledger state is not used: it falsely refuses stored answers such as 8834 and 8851 (Codex r40).\n    85\t\n    86\t### Part B (48 answers)\n    87\tEach answer is read against `probe-targets.json`.\n    88\t- **Evidence targets.** One class each, judged by the claim each cited or pushed entry is used for:\n    89\t  - *intended*: the claim is stated, an entry in `sources` is used for it, and no other entry is used for it without carrying it;\n    90\t  - *unsupported*: stated, with no entry in `sources`. An entry in `partial` alone leaves the exact claim unsupported, but is not a\n    91\t    wrong attribution;\n    92\t  - *misattributed*: an entry is used for the claim while its quote does not carry it. This counts even if the entry supports a\n    93\t    different claim in the same answer, or a correct source is also cited;\n    94\t  - *omitted*: the claim is not stated.\n    95\t  A `markers` search in the answer's text flags the reader's attention; the hand reading decides.\n    96\t- **Placement targets.**\n    97\t  - *correct*: the id appears only in its own field;\n    98\t  - *absent*: not named;\n    99\t  - a substitute evidence id carrying no claim the answer states is *misattributed*.\n   100\t- **Ambiguous targets** (two acceptable sources, or an unclear fact) are reported and count neither way.\n   101\t- **A second, blind reader** (Codex) reads the 48 against the frozen targets. A disagreement goes to a third, source-backed read whose\n   102\t  class decides.\n   103\t\n   104\t### Part C (12 comparisons, all 72 answers read)\n   105\t- Every answer is read in full: its reference, implied outcome, reasons, and the claim each cited or pushed source is used for,\n   106\t  shared sources included.\n   107\t- The numbers only order the reading:\n   108\t  - the sources cited by at least 2 of 3 answers in one arm and at most 1 of 3 in the other;\n   109\t  - any v12 rung outside the range of the v7 rungs.\n   110\t- A v7 answer the request-time check refuses is still read as an answer, and its refusal is recorded (it is the failure the closed\n   111\t  format removes). A comparison is complete with its 3 + 3 answers.\n   112\t\n   113\tA **material harm** is any of these, repeated (in at least 2 of 3 v12 answers and at most 1 of 3 v7 answers):\n   114\t- (a) a source that at least 2 of 3 v7 answers use for a claim that moves the forecast or the reference, which at least 2 of 3 v12\n   115\t  answers neither use nor replace with a listed source carrying that claim;\n   116\t- (b) a source used by at least 2 of 3 v12 answers for a claim its quote does not carry, for example \"Bloomberg supplied the $390\n   117\t  billion forecast\" where Bloomberg's entry gives $393 billion as the market's estimate;\n   118\t- (c) a changed reference or implied outcome whose stated support is a citation of kind (a) or (b);\n   119\t- (d) a v12 rung outside the v7 range whose reasons trace to (a), (b) or (c).\n   120\t\n   121\tA difference that is not repeated, or whose cause cannot be read, is **unresolved**.\n   122\t\n   123\t### Outcome (registered; proposed tolerances for this probe, not historical rates)\n   124\t- **Pass (\"ready to propose adoption\"),** when every condition holds:\n   125\t  - Q1: A1 and A0 return 200 with format-valid answers;\n   126\t  - all 48 Part B answers and all 12 Part C comparisons (72 answers) are complete;\n   127\t  - no v12 answer is refused by the request-time check;\n   128\t  - in Part B, at most 2 of 48 answers have a confirmed misattribution (an answer counts once), with the number of requests affected\n   129\t    and any repetition reported;\n   130\t  - no confirmed material loss in Part B: a material target omitted or unsupported in all 3 draws, which the stored accepted v7\n   131\t    answer to the same request stated and supported;\n   132\t  - no confirmed material harm in Part C.\n   133\t- **Inconclusive:**\n   134\t  - a budget stop, a missing answer, a comparison not started or interrupted;\n   135\t  - an unresolved material loss in Part B: a material target omitted or unsupported in all 3 draws where no stored accepted v7 answer\n   136\t    to that request kept it, as in control-1's 8938, whose retry also dropped 85.4%;\n   137\t  - an unresolved Part C difference that would be material if confirmed.\n   138\t\n   139\tThe outcome is computed by code (`probe_read.py`) from the read classes; the rehearsal exercises it (§4).\n   140\t- **Not ready:** any other failure of the pass conditions, with its reason.\n   141\t- Every outcome goes to Alex; adoption is his decision.\n   142\t\n   143\t## 4. Harness and the $0 rehearsal before the send\n   144\t\n   145\t`probe.py`, harness-only:\n   146\t- it reads `probe-plan.json` and builds each request with `model.build_request`, from poc-v121's setting and v12's format (or v7's\n   147\t  for the control);\n   148\t- it sends through `budget_replay.BudgetTransport` on a ledger at $0.50, in the order and under the start rule of §2;\n   149\t- each answer goes through `model.call`'s format check and the request-time check;\n   150\t- each answer is written to `probe-answers.jsonl` with its part, request, format, draw, the request sha, the cost and the transport's\n   151\t  call number.\n   152\t- `probe_read.py` builds the reading packets: Part B by target, with marker flags; Part C by comparison, with the\n   153\t  symmetric-difference sources and their quotes.\n   154\t\n   155\t**The $0 rehearsal** uses the real budget transport with the provider stubbed underneath (`model.openai_transport` replaced), on a\n   156\tledger file of its own. It must show:\n   157\t- every scheduled send reaches the stub, with a count equal to the plan: 2 + 1 for A, 48 for B, 72 for C;\n   158\t- each request's bytes: system and user equal the stored ones, the format strict and equal to `schema_for('judging-v12', lists)` (or\n   159\t  v7's), the model settings as poc-v121's;\n   160\t- three fresh stub calls per draw set;\n   161\t- the request-time check equals `check_answer` on a ledger fixture whose state is known, accepts every stored committed answer of the\n   162\t  planned requests, and refuses the stored refused ones on their ids;\n   163\t- the stub's valid answer, a format-invalid answer, an HTTP 400 and a 5xx followed by success each take their path;\n   164\t- only one Part C comparison runs at a time;\n   165\t- a ceiling set low blocks a Part C start, and the outcome is \"inconclusive\", never \"pass\";\n   166\t- a retry exhausts the remaining headroom inside an admitted comparison, which stops it; the outcome is \"inconclusive\";\n   167\t- the outcome code's verdicts:\n   168\t  - three Part B answers for control-1's 8938 that state 85.4% with no source are an unresolved material loss, so \"inconclusive\"\n   169\t    (8938 has no stored accepted v7 answer);\n   170\t  - three answers for control-1's 8807 that state the $393 billion reference with no source are a confirmed material loss, so \"not\n   171\t    ready\" (its stored accepted retry 8809 cites e195 and e669);\n   172\t  - three misattributions are \"not ready\";\n   173\t  - a clean set is \"pass\";\n   174\t- the Part B packet for 8938 flags a stub answer that drops \"85.4\".\n   175\t\n   176\tA mutant, Part B sent with the open format, is caught by the byte check.\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"frozen\": \"2026-11-22, before any probe send; read by Claude from the stored refused answers and their requests (probe-plan.json). Amended once before any send (council round 40b): sources completed against the stored accepted retries; a `partial` list (entries that support only a looser version of the claim); misattribution counted by the claim a source is used for. Not edited after the first send.\",\n     3\t \"classes\": {\n     4\t  \"evidence\": \"per target, by the claim each cited or pushed entry is used for: intended (the claim is stated and an entry in `sources` is cited or pushed for it, and no other entry is used for that claim without carrying it); unsupported (stated, with no entry in `sources`; an entry in `partial` alone leaves the exact claim unsupported but is not a wrong attribution); misattributed (an entry is used for the claim while its quote does not carry it, a `near_misses` entry included, even if a correct source is also cited); omitted (not stated)\",\n     5\t  \"placement\": \"correct (the id appears only in its own field: a theme only in theme_pushes, a dependency only in cites_views); absent (not named at all); a substitution (an evidence id cited in its place that carries no claim the answer states) is read as misattributed\"\n     6\t },\n     7\t \"requests\": [\n     8\t  {\n     9\t   \"key\": \"old-1:8326\",\n    10\t   \"view\": \"v5\",\n    11\t   \"targets\": [\n    12\t    {\n    13\t     \"kind\": \"placement\",\n    14\t     \"ids\": [\n    15\t      \"v6\",\n    16\t      \"v7\",\n    17\t      \"v9\"\n    18\t     ],\n    19\t     \"field\": \"theme_pushes\",\n    20\t     \"note\": \"the original pushed them correctly and also cited them in cites\",\n    21\t     \"partial\": []\n    22\t    }\n    23\t   ]\n    24\t  },\n    25\t  {\n    26\t   \"key\": \"old-2:591\",\n    27\t   \"view\": \"v7\",\n    28\t   \"targets\": [\n    29\t    {\n    30\t     \"kind\": \"evidence\",\n    31\t     \"bad\": \"e88\",\n    32\t     \"role\": \"cite\",\n    33\t     \"fact\": \"SemiAnalysis: Kyber NVL144 delayed to 2028 (PCB midplane / circuit board manufacturing)\",\n    34\t     \"passage\": \"The most specific contrary evidence remains SemiAnalysis's report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because the PCB midplane is difficult to manufacture.\",\n    35\t     \"sources\": [\n    36\t      \"e41\",\n    37\t      \"e42\",\n    38\t      \"e120\",\n    39\t      \"e250\"\n    40\t     ],\n    41\t     \"near_misses\": [\n    42\t      \"e85\",\n    43\t      \"e87\"\n    44\t     ],\n    45\t     \"material\": true,\n    46\t     \"markers\": [\n    47\t      \"Kyber\",\n    48\t      \"2028\"\n    49\t     ],\n    50\t     \"why\": \"e88 is an unlisted id the request names in 'same claim as e88' on e42 and e250; the forecast (rung 2) rests on this fact; e41, e42, e120 and e250 each report the SemiAnalysis delay to 2028; NVIDIA's denials (e40, e111, e121, e167, e214, e251) are counterevidence, not sources for this claim\",\n    51\t     \"partial\": []\n    52\t    }\n    53\t   ]\n    54\t  },\n    55\t  {\n    56\t   \"key\": \"old-2:6955\",\n    57\t   \"view\": \"v34\",\n    58\t   \"targets\": [\n    59\t    {\n    60\t     \"kind\": \"evidence\",\n    61\t     \"bad\": \"e3955\",\n    62\t     \"role\": \"push toward_first\",\n    63\t     \"fact\": \"NVIDIA signed a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face (on 2026-09-02, per its SEC filing)\",\n    64\t     \"passage\": \"NVIDIA's SEC filing says it entered into a definitive agreement on 2026-09-02, before the 2027-02-26 deadline\",\n    65\t     \"sources\": [\n    66\t      \"e3768\",\n    67\t      \"e3956\"\n    68\t     ],\n    69\t     \"near_misses\": [\n    70\t      \"e3855\",\n    71\t      \"e3905\",\n    72\t      \"e3915\",\n    73\t      \"e3935\",\n    74\t      \"e3957\",\n    75\t      \"e3958\",\n    76\t      \"e3985\"\n    77\t     ],\n    78\t     \"material\": true,\n    79\t     \"markers\": [\n    80\t      \"definitive agreement\",\n    81\t      \"agreed to acquire\"\n    82\t     ],\n    83\t     \"why\": \"e3955 is not in the request (a garbled number); the push is toward Yes; e3768 (the SEC filing: definitive agreement on September 2) and e3956 (definitive agreement confirmed) carry the exact claim, the stored accepted retry 6979 cites e3768; e3965 (agreed to acquire), e3963 and e3787 support only a looser claim; e2764 and e3554 report no signed agreement yet (older)\",\n    84\t     \"partial\": [\n    85\t      \"e3965\",\n    86\t      \"e3963\",\n    87\t      \"e3787\"\n    88\t     ]\n    89\t    }\n    90\t   ]\n    91\t  },\n    92\t  {\n    93\t   \"key\": \"old-2:7068\",\n    94\t   \"view\": \"v35\",\n    95\t   \"targets\": [\n    96\t    {\n    97\t     \"kind\": \"placement\",\n    98\t     \"ids\": [\n    99\t      \"v10\",\n   100\t      \"v5\",\n   101\t      \"v6\"\n   102\t     ],\n   103\t     \"field\": \"theme_pushes\",\n   104\t     \"partial\": []\n   105\t    }\n   106\t   ]\n   107\t  },\n   108\t  {\n   109\t   \"key\": \"old-2:7774\",\n   110\t   \"view\": \"v35\",\n   111\t   \"targets\": [\n   112\t    {\n   113\t     \"kind\": \"evidence\",\n   114\t     \"bad\": \"e3998\",\n   115\t     \"role\": \"cite and push toward_last\",\n   116\t     \"fact\": \"NVIDIA warned data-center infrastructure shortages could delay deployments\",\n   117\t     \"passage\": \"NVIDIA's capacity constraint and possible infrastructure delays are unquantified\",\n   118\t     \"sources\": [\n   119\t      \"e3993\"\n   120\t     ],\n   121\t     \"near_misses\": [\n   122\t      \"e3994\",\n   123\t      \"e3995\",\n   124\t      \"e3996\"\n   125\t     ],\n   126\t     \"material\": false,\n   127\t     \"markers\": [\n   128\t      \"delay\",\n   129\t      \"infrastructure\"\n   130\t     ],\n   131\t     \"why\": \"e3998 is the same article's next number, not in the request; the tempering fact moves no rung\",\n   132\t     \"partial\": []\n   133\t    }\n   134\t   ]\n   135\t  },\n   136\t  {\n   137\t   \"key\": \"old-3:6236\",\n   138\t   \"view\": \"v23\",\n   139\t   \"targets\": [\n   140\t    {\n   141\t     \"kind\": \"evidence\",\n   142\t     \"bad\": \"e1382\",\n   143\t     \"role\": \"cite\",\n   144\t     \"fact\": \"ambiguous: a supporting detail of the reported NVIDIA-OpenAI financing talks (e1380, pushed toward_first but not cited, is the likeliest)\",\n   145\t     \"passage\": \"The specific reports of discussions to finance up to $350 billion of OpenAI chip purchases make signing some amount by the deadline more likely than not\",\n   146\t     \"sources\": [\n   147\t      \"e1380\",\n   148\t      \"e1385\"\n   149\t     ],\n   150\t     \"near_misses\": [],\n   151\t     \"material\": false,\n   152\t     \"ambiguous\": true,\n   153\t     \"markers\": [],\n   154\t     \"why\": \"e1382 is not in the request; same article as e1380, e1381 (cited), e1385; reported as ambiguous, it counts neither way\",\n   155\t     \"partial\": []\n   156\t    }\n   157\t   ]\n   158\t  },\n   159\t  {\n   160\t   \"key\": \"old-3:6458\",\n   161\t   \"view\": \"v14\",\n   162\t   \"targets\": [\n   163\t    {\n   164\t     \"kind\": \"evidence\",\n   165\t     \"bad\": \"e2769\",\n   166\t     \"role\": \"cite\",\n   167\t     \"fact\": \"NVIDIA expects Vera deployed by every major hyperscaler; shipments underway to lead partners\",\n   168\t     \"passage\": \"the newly disclosed customer plans are supportive but mostly unsized or undated\",\n   169\t     \"sources\": [\n   170\t      \"e3083\"\n   171\t     ],\n   172\t     \"near_misses\": [\n   173\t      \"e2767\",\n   174\t      \"e2768\"\n   175\t     ],\n   176\t     \"material\": false,\n   177\t     \"markers\": [\n   178\t      \"Vera\"\n   179\t     ],\n   180\t     \"why\": \"e2769 is an unlisted id the request names in 'same claim as e2769' on e3083; supportive, unsized\",\n   181\t     \"partial\": []\n   182\t    }\n   183\t   ]\n   184\t  },\n   185\t  {\n   186\t   \"key\": \"control-1:8807\",\n   187\t   \"view\": \"v14\",\n   188\t   \"targets\": [\n   189\t    {\n   190\t     \"kind\": \"evidence\",\n   191\t     \"bad\": \"e393\",\n   192\t     \"role\": \"cite and push toward_first\",\n   193\t     \"fact\": \"the market reference: about $393 billion FY2027 revenue\",\n   194\t     \"passage\": \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3.\",\n   195\t     \"sources\": [\n   196\t      \"e195\",\n   197\t      \"e669\"\n   198\t     ],\n   199\t     \"near_misses\": [],\n   200\t     \"material\": true,\n   201\t     \"markers\": [\n   202\t      \"393\"\n   203\t     ],\n   204\t     \"why\": \"e393 is not in the request; the model wrote the reference amount as an id; e195 (Bloomberg) and e669 (Wall Street average) carry $393 billion\",\n   205\t     \"partial\": []\n   206\t    }\n   207\t   ]\n   208\t  },\n   209\t  {\n   210\t   \"key\": \"control-1:8938\",\n   211\t   \"view\": \"v21\",\n   212\t   \"targets\": [\n   213\t    {\n   214\t     \"kind\": \"evidence\",\n   215\t     \"bad\": \"e362\",\n   216\t     \"role\": \"cite and push toward_first\",\n   217\t     \"fact\": \"Zacks consensus: FY2027 revenue growth 85.4%\",\n   218\t     \"passage\": \"earlier estimates of 83% and 85.4% sit at rung 1\",\n   219\t     \"sources\": [\n   220\t      \"e3620\"\n   221\t     ],\n   222\t     \"near_misses\": [],\n   223\t     \"material\": true,\n   224\t     \"markers\": [\n   225\t      \"85.4\"\n   226\t     ],\n   227\t     \"why\": \"e3620 is the only listed id the first answer left out, and its quote carries 85.4%; e362 drops its last digit\",\n   228\t     \"partial\": []\n   229\t    },\n   230\t    {\n   231\t     \"kind\": \"evidence\",\n   232\t     \"bad\": \"e3111\",\n   233\t     \"role\": \"cite and push toward_last (the retry, 8941)\",\n   234\t     \"fact\": \"ambiguous: an unquantified downside risk (Eisman's price-war warning in e3110, or Burry's customer concentration in e3112)\",\n   235\t     \"passage\": \"Unquantified downside risks do not outweigh the reported results and current-quarter guidance.\",\n   236\t     \"sources\": [\n   237\t      \"e3110\",\n   238\t      \"e3112\"\n   239\t     ],\n   240\t     \"near_misses\": [],\n   241\t     \"material\": false,\n   242\t     \"ambiguous\": true,\n   243\t     \"markers\": [],\n   244\t     \"why\": \"e3111 is not in the request; e3110 and e3112 describe different risks, so the number alone does not settle it (Codex r40)\",\n   245\t     \"partial\": []\n   246\t    }\n   247\t   ]\n   248\t  },\n   249\t  {\n   250\t   \"key\": \"linked-1:8777\",\n   251\t   \"view\": \"v16\",\n   252\t   \"targets\": [\n   253\t    {\n   254\t     \"kind\": \"evidence\",\n   255\t     \"bad\": \"e693\",\n   256\t     \"role\": \"cite\",\n   257\t     \"fact\": \"AMD competition in data-center CPUs (downside risk)\",\n   258\t     \"passage\": \"AMD competition adds downside risk, but does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue\",\n   259\t     \"sources\": [\n   260\t      \"e667\",\n   261\t      \"e694\",\n   262\t      \"e715\",\n   263\t      \"e794\"\n   264\t     ],\n   265\t     \"near_misses\": [\n   266\t      \"e689\"\n   267\t     ],\n   268\t     \"material\": false,\n   269\t     \"markers\": [\n   270\t      \"AMD\"\n   271\t     ],\n   272\t     \"why\": \"e693 is not in the request (an AMD Helios item linked elsewhere); e667 and e694 (AMD share gains), e715 (Gartner: AMD the frontrunner) and e794 (AMD throughput claims against Vera) carry AMD competition; the stored accepted retry 8779 cites e794\",\n   273\t     \"partial\": []\n   274\t    }\n   275\t   ]\n   276\t  },\n   277\t  {\n   278\t   \"key\": \"linked-1:8822\",\n   279\t   \"view\": \"v16\",\n   280\t   \"targets\": [\n   281\t    {\n   282\t     \"kind\": \"placement\",\n   283\t     \"ids\": [\n   284\t      \"v14\"\n   285\t     ],\n   286\t     \"field\": \"cites_views\",\n   287\t     \"partial\": []\n   288\t    }\n   289\t   ]\n   290\t  },\n   291\t  {\n   292\t   \"key\": \"linked-1:8848\",\n   293\t   \"view\": \"v14\",\n   294\t   \"targets\": [\n   295\t    {\n   296\t     \"kind\": \"placement\",\n   297\t     \"ids\": [\n   298\t      \"v5\",\n   299\t      \"v6\"\n   300\t     ],\n   301\t     \"field\": \"theme_pushes\",\n   302\t     \"partial\": []\n   303\t    }\n   304\t   ]\n   305\t  },\n   306\t  {\n   307\t   \"key\": \"linked-1:8855\",\n   308\t   \"view\": \"v16\",\n   309\t   \"targets\": [\n   310\t    {\n   311\t     \"kind\": \"placement\",\n   312\t     \"ids\": [\n   313\t      \"v14\"\n   314\t     ],\n   315\t     \"field\": \"cites_views\",\n   316\t     \"partial\": []\n   317\t    }\n   318\t   ]\n   319\t  },\n   320\t  {\n   321\t   \"key\": \"linked-1:8888\",\n   322\t   \"view\": \"v9\",\n   323\t   \"targets\": [\n   324\t    {\n   325\t     \"kind\": \"placement\",\n   326\t     \"ids\": [\n   327\t      \"v5\",\n   328\t      \"v6\"\n   329\t     ],\n   330\t     \"field\": \"theme_pushes\",\n   331\t     \"partial\": []\n   332\t    }\n   333\t   ]\n   334\t  },\n   335\t  {\n   336\t   \"key\": \"linked-1:8909\",\n   337\t   \"view\": \"v14\",\n   338\t   \"targets\": [\n   339\t    {\n   340\t     \"kind\": \"placement\",\n   341\t     \"ids\": [\n   342\t      \"v5\",\n   343\t      \"v6\"\n   344\t     ],\n   345\t     \"field\": \"theme_pushes\",\n   346\t     \"partial\": []\n   347\t    }\n   348\t   ]\n   349\t  },\n   350\t  {\n   351\t   \"key\": \"linked-1:9021\",\n   352\t   \"view\": \"v21\",\n   353\t   \"targets\": [\n   354\t    {\n   355\t     \"kind\": \"placement\",\n   356\t     \"ids\": [\n   357\t      \"v5\",\n   358\t      \"v6\"\n   359\t     ],\n   360\t     \"field\": \"theme_pushes\",\n   361\t     \"partial\": []\n   362\t    }\n   363\t   ]\n   364\t  }\n   365\t ]\n   366\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'D715|D714' docs/DECISIONS-log.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; ls -l docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe*; nl -ba clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py | sed -n '227,290p'; rg -n '## 0|## 7' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'D715|D714' docs/DECISIONS-log.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; ls -l docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe*; nl -ba clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py | sed -n '227,290p'; rg -n '## 0|## 7' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\"","aggregated_output":"docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:448:**The one next task (D714, 2026-11-21): Alex's choice on the paid acceptance step for judging-v12.** The step: the largest stored\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:454:- **Passed every registered $0 check** (D714; `research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/RESULT.md`):\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10721:## D714 · 2026-11-21 · judging-v12 and poc-v121 built; every registered $0 check passes; the paid acceptance step is Alex's\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10756:## D715 · 2026-11-22 · Alex approves $0.50 for the paid acceptance step of judging-v12\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  1906932 Nov 22 00:36 docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe-plan.json\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff     9944 Nov 22 00:39 docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe-targets.json\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff     7284 Nov 22 00:36 docs/research/2026-11-21-citation-lists/probe_plan.py\n   227\tdef llm_estimate(request):\n   228\t    price_in, price_out = model.PRICES[request['model']]\n   229\t    cap = request.get('max_completion_tokens', request.get('max_tokens'))\n   230\t    if isinstance(cap, bool) or not isinstance(cap, int) or cap <= 0:\n   231\t        raise model.TransportError('request without a positive output cap: refused by the budget')\n   232\t    tokens_in = 2 * len(json.dumps(request).encode('utf-8')) + 4096\n   233\t    return (tokens_in * price_in + cap * price_out) / 1e6\n   234\t\n   235\t\n   236\tdef whole(count):\n   237\t    return isinstance(count, int) and not isinstance(count, bool) and count >= 0\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\tdef llm_cost(response, request):\n   241\t    \"\"\"The answer's cost by `model.call`'s formula; None (settled at the reservation) unless the counts are whole, nonnegative numbers\n   242\t    with some input.\"\"\"\n   243\t    usage = response.get('usage') if isinstance(response, dict) else None\n   244\t    if not isinstance(usage, dict):\n   245\t        return None\n   246\t    tokens_in, tokens_out = usage.get('prompt_tokens'), usage.get('completion_tokens')\n   247\t    details = usage.get('prompt_tokens_details') or {}\n   248\t    cached = (details.get('cached_tokens') or 0) if isinstance(details, dict) else None\n   249\t    if not (whole(tokens_in) and whole(tokens_out) and whole(cached)) or tokens_in == 0:\n   250\t        return None\n   251\t    price_in, price_out = model.PRICES[request['model']]\n   252\t    cached = min(tokens_in, cached)\n   253\t    return (tokens_in - cached) / 1e6 * price_in + cached / 1e6 * price_in * model.CACHED_INPUT_SHARE + tokens_out / 1e6 * price_out\n   254\t\n   255\t\n   256\tdef jev_estimate(request):\n   257\t    price_in, _ = model.PRICES[request['model']]\n   258\t    tokens_in = 2 * len(json.dumps(request, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False).encode('utf-8')) + 4096\n   259\t    return jev.ATTEMPTS * tokens_in * price_in / 1e6\n   260\t\n   261\t\n   262\tclass BudgetTransport:\n   263\t    \"\"\"Called as production calls a transport: (provider, request) for a language model, (request,) for Jev.\"\"\"\n   264\t\n   265\t    def __init__(self, ledger, sleep=time.sleep):\n   266\t        self.ledger, self.sleep = ledger, sleep\n   267\t\n   268\t    def __call__(self, *args):\n   269\t        if len(args) == 1:\n   270\t            return self._jev(args[0])\n   271\t        provider, request = args\n   272\t        for attempt in range(3):\n   273\t            estimate = llm_estimate(request)\n   274\t            if not self.ledger.reserve(estimate):\n   275\t                raise model.TransportError('the shared budget ceiling would be passed: not sent')\n   276\t            try:\n   277\t                response = model.openai_transport(provider, request, max_attempts=1)\n   278\t            except model.TransportError as error:\n   279\t                self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate)\n   280\t                status = getattr(error, 'http_status', None)\n   281\t                if status in CLIENT_ERRORS or attempt == 2 or (status is not None and status != 429 and status < 500):\n   282\t                    raise\n   283\t                self.sleep(5 * (attempt + 1))\n   284\t                continue\n   285\t            except Exception:\n   286\t                self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate)\n   287\t                raise\n   288\t            cost = llm_cost(response, request)\n   289\t            self.ledger.settle(estimate, estimate if cost is None else cost)\n   290\t            return response\n12:## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n168:## 7. Process (no drift)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '12,30p;116,188p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; sed -n '10756,10772p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; rg -n '\"'^def (check_schema|call|build_request|openai_transport|check_answer)'\"' src/hivemind/{model.py,views/judging.py}\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '12,30p;116,188p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; sed -n '10756,10772p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; rg -n '\"'^def (check_schema|call|build_request|openai_transport|check_answer)'\"' src/hivemind/{model.py,views/judging.py}\"","aggregated_output":"## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n## D715 · 2026-11-22 · Alex approves $0.50 for the paid acceptance step of judging-v12\n\n- **The question:** \"引用格式的新版本已经做好，所有不花钱的验证都通过了。要不要花一点钱实测？实测这样做：先发最大的一次请求和一次名单为空的请求，确认服务商接受这个格式；再把当初被退回的 17 个请求和 17 个正常请求用新版本重发，人工对照原文看模型有没有选对证据。每个请求发一次，约 0.10 美元；各发三次看稳不稳，约 0.30 美元。发之前我会先和审查员定好怎么读、怎么判。\"\n  Options: 0.5 美元 (recommended: three sends per request with a margin; a stop above $0.50; the reading rule agreed with the council\n  before the send; Claude reads the results); 0.15 美元 (one send per request); 先不花 (stop; poc-v118 stays the product).\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22): \"0.5 美元 (推荐)\".\n- **Next:** the probe design and its reading rule through the council at $0, the harness dry-run at $0, then the send under a $0.50\n  reservation ledger.\nsrc/hivemind/model.py:116:def build_request(setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, provider):\nsrc/hivemind/model.py:131:def openai_transport(provider, request, timeout=180, max_attempts=3):   # the default preserves the production retry behavior\nsrc/hivemind/model.py:184:def check_schema(value, schema, path='$', root=None):\nsrc/hivemind/model.py:253:def call(archive, cfg, setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, purpose, *, transport=None):\nsrc/hivemind/views/judging.py:259:def check_answer(conn, view_id, data, request_text=None, lists=None):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
